# banner.view: ONE band, full-colour background, rounded Powerline caps.
#
# THREE ways in, cheapest first.
#
# 1. A decorator over a marked quote. Nothing to create, and the fallback where this
#    hook does not run is a native alert box:
#
#      @{view:banner}
#      > [!WARNING]
#      > two flaky suites, publication is blocked
#
#    The marker is `[!TOKEN]` alone on the first line, ONE uppercase run: no space, no
#    glyph, no second word. Anything wider is not a marker, it is the first line of the
#    content, and it prints inside the band where you can see it. The quote must be
#    followed by a BLANK LINE or end the message: prose on the very next line joins the
#    zone, no parser claims the mixture, and the raw text shows instead.
#
# 2. Your OWN vocabulary: shadow this file from an earlier dir on the search path and
#    change the @text table below. You patch nothing in the package, and the order of
#    viewsPath is the whole mechanism.
#
# 3. A fenced block, for a one-off the table has no entry for. The extra lines are the
#    honest price of writing presentation in a message:
#
#      ```view:banner
#      type: deploy
#      content: shipped to staging
#      ```
#
# The COLOUR comes from the tone slot, never from the table: the table holds the WORD, so
# a kind has one place to declare its look and one to declare its name. `type` picks it
# when the palette knows the word (`warning` is yellow), `tone:` on the decorator
# overrides it outright, and anything the palette does not know falls to the @tone below.
# SEVERAL bands are several blocks, never one list: the caps exist to make ONE band read
# as a pill, and stacking them scallops both flanks.
#
# The caps are U+E0B6/U+E0B4 (Powerline). A font without them shows two hollow boxes
# around the band; swap them for ▐ and ▌ if yours does not. They spend {{tone_cap}},
# which the engine DERIVES from the chip filling the band, so they match it exactly
# whatever the class and whatever the theme. Spending {{tone}} there is off by one shade
# and was the bug: bold promotes a base-sixteen foreground to the bright slot, nothing
# promotes a background. Every COLOUR fills a band: the severities (warning, error,
# success, info, warn, fail, pass, high, med, low) and the plain names alike (red, green,
# yellow, cyan, orange, gold, key, dim). What has no chip is furniture (`code`, `box_rule`)
# and a host's own foreground tag: name one and the band vanishes, leaving two solid caps
# around bare text. Do not.
#
# The band is emitted on ONE line and nothing measures it: a body wider than the terminal
# is soft-wrapped by the terminal itself, the colour continues on the next row and the
# closing cap lands at the end of the last one. That is the accepted look, not an oversight.
@tone chip
@text kinds note="ⓘ NOTE" tip="✔ TIP" important="★ IMPORTANT" warning="⚠ WARNING" caution="⛔ CAUTION" error="✖ ERROR" success="✔ SUCCESS" info="ⓘ INFO" warn="⚠ WARN" fail="✖ FAIL" pass="✔ PASS" high="▲ HIGH" med="■ MED" low="▼ LOW" version="◆ VERSION" *="ⓘ NOTE"
{{tone_cap}}{{/}}{{tone_bg}} ${type:kinds}  ${content} {{/}}{{tone_cap}}{{/}}
